course: geography
lesson: 27
title: Resources and what countries trade
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Britain cannot grow bananas outdoors, and Ecuador does not build many jet engines, so each sells what it is good at and buys the rest. That is trade, and it is why a lunchbox can hold food from five continents.
explain: Nature's useful things, its resources, are scattered unevenly: oil pools under a few regions, lithium and cobalt for phone batteries are dug in a few countries, rivers and good soil are where they are. Places that hold rare resources become important, and sometimes the targets of greed.
explain: Most goods now cross the sea in standard steel boxes on giant ships, squeezing through a few narrow passages called chokepoints, like the Strait of Malacca or the Suez Canal. Block one, and shops on the other side of the world feel it.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 each location produces one raw material and trade flows to the markets that pull hardest. Real places produced many things at once, and real money also came from services such as shipping insurance and banking, which no map shows.
explain: History has its own resource rush: silver from Potosí in Bolivia and from Mexico flowed to Europe and across the Pacific to China, dug under the forced-labour mita in which many Indigenous Andean workers died.
fact: Natural resources are useful things from nature, such as water, soil, wood, fish, metals, oil and gas.
fact: Some resources, such as wood or fish, can renew themselves if used carefully, while others, such as oil and metals, do not come back once used up.
fact: Countries trade because none can make everything well, so each sells what it is good at producing and buys the rest.
fact: Much of the world's oil comes from a few places, such as the Middle East, the USA, Russia and Venezuela, because oil formed long ago from the remains of tiny sea creatures trapped under special rocks only in some places.
fact: Oil mattered little before the 1800s, when the main fuels were wood, charcoal and, later, coal.
fact: Lithium and cobalt, needed for batteries in phones and electric cars, are mined in a few countries such as Australia, Chile and the Democratic Republic of the Congo.
fact: Silver from Potosí in Bolivia and from Mexico flowed to Europe and across the Pacific to China in the 1500s to 1700s, because China wanted silver and paid for it with silk, tea and porcelain.
fact: The Potosí mine was worked under the Spanish mita forced-labour system, in which many Indigenous Andean workers died.
fact: Most of the world's goods now travel in standard steel containers, all one size so cranes, ships, trains and lorries can handle them fast, on huge ships, the biggest of which carry over 20,000 containers.
fact: A few narrow sea passages, called chokepoints, carry a huge share of world trade, such as the Strait of Malacca, the Suez Canal, the Strait of Hormuz and the Panama Canal.
fact: Exports are goods a country sells abroad and imports are goods it buys from abroad.
fact: Countries also trade services, such as banking, insurance, software, films and tourism, not only goods.
fact: Farmers who grow crops like cocoa and coffee often earn very little of the price paid in shops, because most of it goes to traders, factories, brands and shops.
exercise: What are natural resources? | Useful things from nature, like water, wood and metals | Things that are made only in factories, like cars and phones | Money kept in a national bank | Goods bought from other countries
exercise: Which of these resources can renew itself if used carefully? | Fish | Crude oil | Iron ore | Natural gas
exercise: Why do countries trade? | None can make everything well, so each sells and buys | Trade is required of every country by the old rules of the sea | Every country can make everything but is lazy | Only to get rid of things nobody wants
exercise: Which of these is a major oil region? | The Middle East | Iceland | New Zealand | The Swiss Alps and Austria
exercise: What were the main fuels before the 1800s? | Wood and charcoal | Oil, coal and petrol | Natural gas and uranium | Electricity and batteries
exercise: What are lithium and cobalt needed for today? | Batteries for phones and electric cars | Making clear glass for windows | Feeding farm animals in winter | Painting houses and bridges so they do not rust
exercise: Which country is a big source of cobalt? | The Democratic Republic of the Congo | Iceland | The Republic of Ireland | The Grand Duchy of Luxembourg
exercise: Where did American silver flow in the 1500s to 1700s? | To Europe and across the Pacific to China | Only to Africa | Back underground into new mines | To Australia and New Zealand, to buy their wool
exercise: How was the Potosí silver mine worked? | Under the mita forced-labour system | By free volunteers who were paid in gold | By machines powered by steam | By miners brought from China
exercise: How do most of the world's goods travel today? | In standard steel containers on huge ships | In wooden barrels on sailing ships | By aeroplane, because planes are now the cheapest way | On camels along the Silk Road
exercise: What is a chokepoint? | A narrow sea passage carrying much trade | A port where ships are built | A mine high up in the mountains where the air is thin | A border post on a road
exercise: Which of these is a chokepoint? | The Strait of Malacca | The middle of the Pacific | The middle of the Sahara | Lake Victoria in Africa
exercise: What is an export? | A good a country sells abroad | A good a country buys from abroad | A tax paid on all imports at the border | A good made and sold at home only
exercise: What is an import? | A good a country buys from abroad | A good a country sells abroad | A good that is not allowed in | A good that is found only deep in mines
exercise: Which of these is a service a country can export? | Insurance | Coffee beans | Iron ore from Sweden | Bananas from Ecuador
exercise: How much of a chocolate bar's shop price do cocoa farmers often get? | Very little of it | Nearly every bit of it | Exactly half of it | All of it plus a bonus
test: Why does Britain import bananas? | Bananas need a tropical climate that Britain lacks | Britain bans growing bananas by law to protect the farmers of its old colonies | British bananas are too big to eat | Bananas in Britain grow only in winter
test: A huge ship gets stuck sideways in the Suez Canal for a week. What happens? | Hundreds of ships are delayed and goods arrive late | Nothing, the other ships simply sail round it on the wide canal | The canal moves to a new place | All trade between countries stops forever
test: In the game each location makes one good. Is that how a real region worked? | No, real places produced many things at once | Yes, each town made exactly one thing and bought all the rest | Yes, and they swapped it every year | No, real places produced nothing at all
test: Why did American silver end up in China? | China wanted silver and sold silk, tea and porcelain for it | China owned the silver mines in Bolivia | Silver floated across the Pacific on the strong ocean currents to China | Chinese ships dug it up in Mexico
test: oil's in Saudi but not in my back garden, why's that | Oil formed from ancient sea life under special rocks in some places | Oil is spread evenly but hidden deeper in Britain | Kings decided where to put the oil | Oil only forms in hot countries today
test: Why do navies care so much about narrow straits? | Whoever controls them can block a huge share of trade | Straits are the only places in the sea where fish can live and breed | Navies need narrow water to park ships | Straits are where all the oil is pumped up
test: My uncle says London earns money without making stuff. How? | It sells services like banking and insurance | It prints money and keeps it | Other countries pay it for being old | He is wrong, every pound comes from factories
test: If every country tried to make absolutely everything itself, what would happen? | Many things would be dearer or worse, as each would make things it is bad at | Everything would become much cheaper, because there would be no more transport costs at all | Nothing would change at all | Every country would grow bananas easily
test: Why are lithium mines so important today? | Batteries for phones and electric cars need lithium | Lithium is used to make bread rise in factory bakeries | Lithium is the main fuel of ships | Lithium mines produce clean water
test: Chocolate costs a lot, but the cocoa farmer is poor. How can that be? | Most of the price goes to traders, factories, brands and shops | Cocoa farmers refuse to sell their cocoa | Chocolate is made without cocoa now | Farmers are paid only in chocolate
test: Why are containers so useful for trade? | They are all one size, so cranes, ships, trains and lorries handle them fast | They float on their own without a ship, so they can be towed in long lines across the sea | They keep everything inside warm | Each one is a different shape for each good
test: In 1511 and 1515 the Portuguese seized Malacca and Hormuz. What were they after? | Control of chokepoints on the trade routes | Fresh water for their farms | Lithium for batteries | Good land for planting wheat
exam: Identify the principal reason for which states engage in international trade. | No state can produce all goods efficiently, so each exchanges what it produces best | International law compels each state to trade a fixed and equal quantity of goods each year | Each state produces all goods efficiently but wishes to dispose of surplus | Trade is conducted solely for the purpose of collecting customs duties
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a maritime chokepoint? | A narrow passage of the sea through which a large proportion of trade must pass | A port in which merchant vessels are constructed and repaired | A region of the open ocean far removed from any land | A mine situated in a mountainous region near the coast
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the trade in services. | States export services such as banking, insurance, software and tourism | Only tangible goods may be exported between states | Services are consumed exclusively within the state that produces them, and never abroad | The export of services is prohibited by international agreement
exam: It is given that silver was extracted at Potosí between the sixteenth and eighteenth centuries. Ascertain which statement is correct. | It was worked under forced labour and much of the silver reached Europe and China | It was worked by salaried volunteers and the silver remained in Bolivia | It was worked by steam machinery and the silver was exported to Australia | It was worked by Chinese miners and the silver was buried again
